/*
    To semi-formalize the English Statement into a structure of premises and conclusions, we will examine the English Statement sentence by sentence, and break down each sentence into natural language clauses that are as atomic as possible.
*/

{
    "premises": [
        /*
        The sentence "Line WY and SZ intersect at X, TV and SZ intersect at U." first declares 3 lines: WY, SZ, TV.

        From the instructions, we know that all points and lines distinct, so it also declares 6 distinct points on 3 distinct lines: W, Y on line WY, S, Z on line SZ, T, V on line TV.

        It then states intersection relations between these 3 lines: WY and SZ intersect at point X, TV and SZ intersect at point U.

        Therefore, we can break down the sentence into the following 5 premises:
        */
        "W and Y are distinct points on line WY",
        "S and Z are distinct points on line SZ",
        "T and V are distinct points on line TV",
        "The distinct lines WY and SZ intersect at point X",
        "The distinct lines TV and SZ intersect at point U",
        /*
        For now, the premises above only partially express the fact that the points W, Y, S, Z, T, V are all distinct, and the lines WY, SZ, TV are all distinct.

        However, we might be able to incorporate and express the rest distinctness relations in later premises, so we don't create additional premises for them right now.

        Instead, we will first examine all sentences in the English statement and eventually create premises for the rest unexpressed distinctness relations if necessary.
        */

        /*
        The sentence "The points S, U, X, Z are collinear and ordered." states an order relation between the 4 points S, U, X, Z: they are on the same line SZ and ordered in this sequence.

        For this order relation, if we don't explicitly state the distinctness of the points, it might be interpreted as X can be the same point as S or Z, which is not what we want.

        Therefore, we can incorporate the fact that the points X, Z, U are mutually distinct, and the points U, S, X are mutually distinct.

        Therefore, we have the following premise:
        */
        "The distinct points S, U, X, Z are collinear and ordered in this sequence from S to Z",
        /*
        Since we had explicitly expressed that S and Z are distinct points on line SZ, we also expressed the fact that the points S, U, X, Z are all mutally distinct.
        */

        /*
        The sentences "The points T, W are on one side of line SZ. The points V, Y are on the other side of line SZ." state same-side / opposing-side relations between the points T, W, V, and Y with respect to line SZ: T and W are on the same side of line SZ, V and Y are on the same side of line SZ, T and V are on opposing sides of line SZ, W and Y are on opposing sides of line SZ.

        For the same-side relation, if we don't explicitly state the distinctness of the points, it might be interpreted as T and W can be the same point. Also, if we don't explicitly state the non-collinearity of these points, it might be interpreted that at least one of T and W lie on line SZ, which is not what we want.

        Therefore, we can incorporate the fact that the points T and W are mutually distinct, and that they don't lie on line SZ.

        Similarly, we can incorporate the fact that the points V and Y are mutually distinct, and that they don't lie on line SZ.

        For the opposing-side relation, if we don't explicitly state the distinctness of the points, it might be interpreted that one of T and V can lie on line SZ being the same as point U, which is not what we want.

        Therefore, we can incorporate the fact that the points T and V are both not on line SZ.

        Similarly, we can incorporate the fact that the points W and Y are both not on line SZ.

        Therefore, we can break it down into the following 4 premises:
        */
        "The distinct points T and W are on the same side of line SZ, both not on line SZ",
        "The distinct points V and Y are on the same side of line SZ, both not on line SZ",
        "The distinct points T and V are on opposing sides of line SZ, both not on line SZ",
        "The distinct points W and Y are on opposing sides of line SZ, both not on line SZ",
        /*
        Since we had explicitly expressed that T and V are distinct points on line TV, and W and Y are distinct points on line WY, we also expressed the fact that the points T, W, V, Y are all mutally distinct.

        Since we had explicitly expressed that distinct points S, U, X, Z are on line SZ, and distinct points T, W, V, Y are not on line SZ, we also expressed the fact that S, U, X, Z, T, W, V, Y are all mutally distinct.

        Now, we have checked that all distinctness relations among the points T, W, V, Y, S, U, X, Z are expressed. There is only one more distinctness relation left to check: the lines WY and TV are mutually distinct.

        If WY were the same line as TV, then they should intersect at the same point with SZ, which makes X and U the same point. Since we had expressed that X and U are distinct points, we also expressed the fact that the lines WY and TV are mutually distinct.

        Therefore, we checked that all distinctness relations among points and lines are expressed.
        */

        /*
        The sentence "Given ∠ U X W and ∠ T U X are supplementary." states a metric relation between the angles ∠ U X W and ∠ T U X.

        Therefore, we have the following premise:
        */
        "The angles ∠ U X W and ∠ T U X are supplementary"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
        /*
        The sentence "Complete the proof that W Y ∥ T V." states the goal of the proof: to show that lines WY and TV are parallel.

        Therefore, we have the following conclusion:
        */
        "The lines WY and TV are parallel, denoted as W Y ∥ T V"
    ]
}
